The wave theory predicts that energy arrives continuously and is shared among many electrons, so:
- any frequency should work if the light is intense enough or shone long enough, but experiments show a **threshold frequency**;
- the maximum kinetic energy should increase with intensity, but it depends only on the **frequency**;
- dim light should need time to build up enough energy before an electron leaves, but emission is **immediate**.
The photon model explains all three: one photon gives all its energy $hf$ to one electron, so emission needs $hf \geq \phi$; the energy available depends on $f$ alone; and the electron leaves at once. A brighter beam has more photons per second, so more electrons per second, but each photon still has energy $hf$.
